 This crate provides a fully written in Rust memory allocator for Vulkan, DirectX 12 and Metal.
 
+## Cached memory and allocation failures
+
+Each memory type retains at most one empty shared block, bounded by the smaller of twice
+its configured minimum block size and its maximum. Vulkan also bounds the combined empty
+cache of types sharing a heap by that heap's size. These limits do not represent an OS
+memory budget. Partially occupied blocks remain live.
+
+Backends reclaim empty caches on recoverable allocation failure and retry. If preferred
+shared-block growth still fails, they can use a smaller block that fits the resource,
+including below the configured minimum. Vulkan and D3D12 reduce shared-block sizes
+geometrically down to the resource floor (aligned to 4 MiB for D3D12), retaining useful
+sharing capacity under pressure. Metal tries the resource floor directly because heap
+creation failure provides no reason code. Fatal backend errors are returned immediately.
+
+`Allocator::cached_capacity()` reports empty cached capacity. `trim()` releases all of it;
+`trim_to(bytes)` releases whole cached blocks, largest first, until at most `bytes` remain.
+Both return the capacity removed. They preserve live allocations and do not wait for GPU
+work. External heap references can delay actual native memory release.
+
+## Rust version
+
+The allocator core supports Rust 1.71 with a compatible dependency lockfile. Tested
+dependency versions include thiserror 2.0.20, windows 0.58 and hashbrown 0.16.1. Newer
+versions allowed by the dependency ranges can require a newer compiler; select compatible
+versions in your lockfile when using Rust 1.71. The optional `visualizer` feature uses egui
+versions declaring Rust 1.72 or later. Development dependencies can require newer Rust too.
+
